The Judicial Power of the United States
The United States Constitution grants three primary powers to the federal government: legislative (Article I), executive (Article II), and judicial (Article III). The Constitution established a central government of limited and enumerated powers, and just as the powers of Congress are enumerated and limited by Article I, and the powers of the President are enumerated and limited by Article II, the scope of judicial power is enumerated and limited by Article III.

Article III prescribes the “judicial Power of the United States.” It defines the types of courts that the federal government can create and what the federal government can empower those courts to do. For this reason, federal courts are courts of limited jurisdiction. They cannot simply decide any case that comes before them. Rather, they have jurisdiction only to decide cases that fall within the scope of the power granted them by Article III. And because Article III gives Congress the power to create (or not create) lower federal courts, the lower federal courts have no jurisdiction except the jurisdiction granted to them by statute. In this chapter, we will study some of the federal statutes that grant jurisdiction to the federal district courts.

When a case is filed in federal court, the federal court, before it can decide the case, must first determine whether the case falls within the subject matter jurisdiction of the court. If it does not, the federal court cannot decide the case. It must dismiss the case for lack of subject matter jurisdiction.

In contrast, Article III says nothing about state courts. This is an important distinction. State courts pre-existed the U.S. Constitution. The powers of state courts are the product of state constitutions and state legislation. When ratified in 1789, the U.S. Constitution was silent on the powers of state courts. Only after the Civil War, with the ratification of the Reconstruction Amendments (the 13th, 14th, and 15th Amendments), did the U.S. Constitution begin to limit the power of state courts. (We have studied some of these limitations in Chapter 2 and will encounter more in Chapter 11.) This means that state courts are, by default, courts of general jurisdiction. State courts can hear any type of case that comes before them unless their jurisdiction is taken away by statute or by state constitution. The baseline, then, is that a state court can hear any case that comes before it.

Before turning to the caselaw on subject matter jurisdiction, we begin with the ground upon which the jurisdiction of the federal courts rests: Article III.

