NOTE ON ARTICLE III
There are at least two easily overlooked features of Article III that are essential to understanding subject matter jurisdiction. First, the Constitution establishes only a United States Supreme Court. The task of creating “inferior” federal courts is left to Congress.

Second, Article III distinguishes between “original” and “appellate” jurisdiction. Appellate jurisdiction, as the name implies, is jurisdiction to review the decision of another court. Original jurisdiction refers to the power to hear a case in the first instance. The Constitution assigns original jurisdiction to the Supreme Court in only a small set of cases within the federal judicial power.

Query: If Article III creates only a Supreme Court, but assigns it only appellate jurisdiction in most cases within the federal judicial power, which courts have original jurisdiction? To what extent can Congress determine the answer to this question?

Pursuant to its power to establish inferior federal courts under Article III, Congress has established 91 district courts corresponding to geographic jurisdictions (89 within the 50 states, and one each in the District of Columbia and Puerto Rico) and the Court of International Trade, which has specialized jurisdiction over certain claims related to customs and international trade. These 92 courts exercise original jurisdiction over claims within the federal judicial power. Congress has established 12 regional circuit courts of appeals (eleven numbered circuits and the Court of Appeals for the District of Columbia Circuit), which have appellate jurisdiction over cases in the federal district courts, and the Federal Circuit, which has appellate jurisdiction over certain specialized cases, including cases in the Court of International Trade. The jurisdiction of each of these courts is defined by statute.

Article III describes the maximum scope of jurisdiction beyond which Congress cannot expand the federal courts’ power. As the Supreme Court made clear in Marbury v. Madison, 5 U.S. (1 Cranch) 137 (1803), which we read in Chapter 1, a statutory grant of jurisdiction that is greater than the judicial power outlined by Article III conflicts with Article III and is therefore invalid.

With respect to the inferior federal courts, Congress need not grant them the full extent of jurisdiction permitted by the Constitution. Thus, it is not
